  "delta_attributions": [
    "iter_0 (cs0.4) is the combine top-1 leader: eval R-p@1=0.795/@3=0.688/@5=0.628/@10=0.522/@20=0.458, but train_val_gap=0.038>0.02 (mild overfit, val_brier 0.131 vs train 0.094) and anti_auc_flag=unknown. Pivoting iter_1 to a structurally-different regularizer family (split-quality gamma) per playbook rules 6+9. Scout/combine evidence: gamma is the dominant NO-OVERFIT lever here -- gamma0.5 standalone gave eval R-p@1=0.790 (matches cs0.4's top-1) with val_brier=0.114 (vs 0.131) and gap=-0.001 (vs +0.038), i.e. same top-of-book at a clean fit; gamma1.0 DOMINATES the @3-@20 deep tail (0.708/0.696/0.609/0.533, gap=-0.008). Setting colsample_bytree back to 1.0 + gamma=0.5 to test pure gamma0.5 (the cs0.4+gamma STACK regressed top-1 to 0.54 in combine, so NOT stacking -- playbook rule 8). No feature prune: gap is mild and the 5 sub-floor features are redundant not noise (rule 2), FS is neutral-to-harmful on this near-balanced model. Oracle = eval R-p@1 then @3; val_brier is misaligned (gamma1.0 best val_brier but lower @1) so reading eval R-p@1 as primary.",
    "iter_1 (gamma0.5) gave eval R-p@1=0.790/@3=0.660/@5=0.594/@10=0.517 with no_overfit=True (gap=-0.0008, val_brier=0.114) -- a clean fit matching iter_0 (cs0.4) at top-1 (0.795) but slightly below it at @3/@5/@10. iter_0 cs0.4 still leads top-of-book but has a mild overfit gap (0.038). Per playbook rule 9 (map >=2 values on a knob before pivoting), iter_2 moves UP the gamma response curve to gamma=1.0 -- the combine showed gamma1.0 DOMINATES the @3-@20 deep tail (0.708/0.696/0.609/0.533) with the best val_brier (0.112) and no overfit (gap=-0.008), trading ~0.06 top-1 for large @3-@20 gains. This maps the gamma curve {0.5,1.0} and tests whether the deep-tail-strong regime is preferable to cs0.4's top-1-strong regime. colsample stays 1.0. Oracle = eval R-p@1 then @3."
  ],
